Aight, listen up! 🎤 Let me break down **Dependency Injection (DI) in C#** — gangster style. No fluff, just straight talk from the streets of Software City. 😤

---

## 🔫 The Problem: Tight Coupling (Being a Control Freak)

Imagine you're a mob boss (a C# class), and you need a getaway driver. But instead of calling someone, **you go build your own car from scratch every single time.** That's wild, right?

That's what **tight coupling** looks like in code:

```csharp
public class MobBoss
{
    private GetawayDriver _driver = new GetawayDriver(); // Building the car yourself. Rookie move.

    public void PullHeist()
    {
        _driver.Drive();
    }
}
```

Here, your `MobBoss` class is tightly coupled with `GetawayDriver`. If tomorrow you want to replace the `GetawayDriver` with a `HelicopterPilot`, you'll have to go in and modify the `MobBoss` class code. That's bad for business, fam.

---

## 💰 The Solution: Dependency Injection (Let Someone Else Handle It)

A real OG doesn't build their own tools — **they get supplied**. Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern that allows objects to receive their dependencies from an external source rather than creating them internally. Instead of hardcoding dependencies within a class, dependencies are "injected" into the class from the outside.

Think of it like this: If the mob boss goes *directly* to the chop shop to get a car every time — that's tight coupling. Instead, the *organization* delivers the car to the boss — that's Dependency Injection. Now the boss only focuses on the heist, not sourcing the wheels.

---

## 🕶️ The Three Ways to Get Your Supplies (Types of DI)

In C#, there are 3 types of DI: **Constructor Injection**, **Property Injection**, and **Method Injection**.

---

### 1. 🏗️ Constructor Injection (The Standard Drop-Off)
**Most common.** Your crew delivers everything you need *before* the job even starts.

```csharp
public interface IGetawayDriver
{
    void Drive();
}

public class CarDriver : IGetawayDriver
{
    public void Drive() => Console.WriteLine("Burnin' rubber, boss!");
}

public class HelicopterPilot : IGetawayDriver
{
    public void Drive() => Console.WriteLine("We flyin' out, boss!");
}

public class MobBoss
{
    private readonly IGetawayDriver _driver;

    // The driver is INJECTED — boss don't build nothin' himself
    public MobBoss(IGetawayDriver driver)
    {
        _driver = driver;
    }

    public void PullHeist()
    {
        _driver.Drive();
    }
}
```

The key move here is that the constructor parameter is of the **interface type**, not a concrete class. This means it can accept *any* concrete class object that implements the interface. Swap `CarDriver` for `HelicopterPilot` without touching `MobBoss`. Smooth. 😎

---

### 2. 🪟 Property Injection (The Dead Drop)
The supplies show up *after* the object is created, left at a designated spot.

```csharp
public class MobBoss
{
    public IGetawayDriver Driver { get; set; } // Set it from outside

    public void PullHeist() => Driver.Drive();
}
```

Dependencies are exposed as public properties and set externally. While this offers flexibility, it may make it less clear what dependencies a class requires.

---

### 3. 🤝 Method Injection (The Hand-Off)
The supplies are handed over only when needed for *one specific job*.

```csharp
public class MobBoss
{
    public void PullHeist(IGetawayDriver driver) // Gets the driver just for this job
    {
        driver.Drive();
    }
}
```

Method injection involves passing dependencies through methods. This approach is useful when a dependency is required for a specific method but not for the entire lifetime of the object.

---

## 🏦 The DI Container (Your Crime Syndicate's Logistics Operation)

Running this all manually is small-time. Real bosses let **the Organization** (the DI Container) handle all the logistics. The framework takes on the responsibility of creating an instance of the dependency and disposing of it when it's no longer needed.

You register your crew in `Program.cs` like putting people on the payroll:

```csharp
// Register your crew
services.AddTransient<IGetawayDriver, CarDriver>();
```

And then the DI Container automatically hands the right driver to the boss — no questions asked.

---

## ⏳ Service Lifetimes (How Long Your Crew Sticks Around)

When registering services, you can specify their lifetimes:
- **Transient**: A new instance is provided *every time* the service is requested.
- **Scoped**: A single instance is provided *per request*.
- **Singleton**: A single instance is created and *shared throughout the application's lifetime*.

```csharp
services.AddTransient<IGetawayDriver, CarDriver>();   // New driver every job
services.AddScoped<IGetawayDriver, CarDriver>();      // Same driver per mission
services.AddSingleton<IGetawayDriver, CarDriver>();   // ONE driver for life
```

---

## 🎯 Why Go This Route?

By using the DI pattern, your class doesn't depend on a concrete type — only the **interface** it implements. This design makes it easy to change the implementation without modifying the class itself.

It also reduces the coupling between classes, making it easier to manage and maintain code.

---

**Bottom line?** Don't be a lone wolf who builds everything himself. Let the Organization (DI Container) supply your crew, keep things flexible, and your code will stay clean, testable, and untouchable. 🤌💼
